{"name":"Shōji","hex":"#ded5c7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"Shoji","blocks":[{"text":"A shoji  is a door, window or room divider used in traditional Japanese architecture, consisting of translucent sheets on a lattice frame. Where light transmission is not needed, the similar but opaque fusuma is used. Shoji usually slide, but may occasionally be hung or hinged, especially in more rustic styles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Gs01-rinsyun.jpg/330px-Gs01-rinsyun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoji","wikidata":"Q1069525"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Gs02-rinsyun.jpg/500px-Gs02-rinsyun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Gs02-rinsyun.jpg/500px-Gs02-rinsyun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Shoji doors next to the tokonoma alcove, Rinshunkaku","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGs02-rinsyun.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Fukiya_katayama_house03s3200.jpg/500px-Fukiya_katayama_house03s3200.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Fukiya_katayama_house03s3200.jpg/1280px-Fukiya_katayama_house03s3200.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Fukiya_katayama_house03s3200.jpg/500px-Fukiya_katayama_house03s3200.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A tatami room surrounded by paper shoji (paper outside, lattice inside). The shoji are surrounded by an engawa (porch/corridor); the engawa is surrounded by garasu-do, all-glass sliding pa